WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:00.963
I've never been able

00:00:00.963 --> 00:00:03.030
to found a single country in
the world where the policies

00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:05.070
that have been advocated for
blacks in the United States

00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:07.420
have lifted any people out of poverty.

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:09.460
I've seen many examples
around the world about

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:11.269
people began in poverty
and ended in affluence,

00:00:11.269 --> 00:00:15.480
not one of them has
followed any pattern at all

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:17.470
like what is being advocated for blacks

00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:18.730
in the United States.

00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:20.879
That's the economist and
social critic Thomas Sowell

00:00:20.879 --> 00:00:24.250
critiquing affirmative
action back in 1981.

00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:27.050
In an age in which
espousing unpopular ideas

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:29.589
can destroy the careers of
journalists and academics.

00:00:29.589 --> 00:00:32.060
Sowell's candor and insistence

00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:35.120
on following the facts wherever they lead,

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:37.713
are more worthy of appreciation than ever.

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:41.590
Sowell was born in rural
North Carolina in 1930

00:00:41.590 --> 00:00:44.160
to an uneducated family
without electricity

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:45.470
or running water.

00:00:45.470 --> 00:00:47.610
He was orphaned and
raised by a great aunt.

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:49.413
When he was eight his
family moved to Harlem

00:00:49.413 --> 00:00:51.900
and at one point he
was living in a shelter

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:54.563
for homeless boys keeping
a knife under his pillow

00:00:54.563 --> 00:00:55.970
for protection.

00:00:55.970 --> 00:00:57.943
He didn't finish college until he was 28

00:00:57.943 --> 00:01:00.350
and went on to receive his PhD

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:02.310
from the University of Chicago

00:01:02.310 --> 00:01:04.470
before he became
internationally celebrated

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:06.270
as an intellectual and writer.

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:09.290
His upbringing was more than
something to be overcome,

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:11.660
it profoundly shaped his scholarship.

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:14.180
In particular by making him disdainful

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:17.343
of elite intellectuals who
impose their ideas on society

00:01:17.343 --> 00:01:18.970
through government.

00:01:18.970 --> 00:01:20.623
As Sowell wrote in his memoir,

00:01:20.623 --> 00:01:23.090
"Growing up he had daily
contact with people

00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:26.230
who were neither well educated
nor particularly genteel,

00:01:26.230 --> 00:01:29.520
but who had practical wisdom
far beyond what I had."

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:30.353
That gave him,

00:01:30.353 --> 00:01:34.110
"A lasting respect for the
common sense of ordinary people,

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:36.960
a factor routinely ignored
by the intellectuals

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:39.760
among whom I would later make my career.

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:42.690
Sowell provocative body
of work on everything

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:44.470
from race to childhood development

00:01:44.470 --> 00:01:46.160
to Charter Schools and Their Enemies,

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:47.825
is the subject of a new documentary

00:01:47.825 --> 00:01:51.190
and a forthcoming biography
titled 'Maverick'.

00:01:51.190 --> 00:01:53.150
He is someone who has been something

00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:56.010
of an intellectual loner
throughout his career.

00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:57.760
Jason Riley is a senior fellow

00:01:57.760 --> 00:01:58.793
at the Manhattan Institute,

00:01:58.793 --> 00:02:02.120
a columnist at the Wall Street
Journal and the author behind

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:04.700
the new documentary and
biography of Sowell.

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:06.533
Someone who has bucked convention,

00:02:06.533 --> 00:02:09.260
concerned himself with
being a truth teller,

00:02:09.260 --> 00:02:10.230
being a straight shooter.

00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:12.590
Maybe this is a commentary
on intellectuals today

00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:14.260
as that makes him a Maverick.

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:17.330
Someone who puts truth about popularity,

00:02:17.330 --> 00:02:20.370
who doesn't concern himself
with being politically correct.

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:21.960
He follows the facts where they lead,

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:24.600
whether or not the
conclusion is a popular one.

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:26.332
Riley says that Sowell's genius rests

00:02:26.332 --> 00:02:30.200
in his unwavering attention
to empirical facts and data

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:33.630
and policy outcomes
regardless of intentions.

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:36.427
A tendency that was
sharpened by his PhD training

00:02:36.427 --> 00:02:38.027
at the University of Chicago

00:02:38.027 --> 00:02:41.230
under future Nobel prize
winners Milton Friedman,

00:02:41.230 --> 00:02:43.710
George Stigler and Friedrich Hayek.

00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:44.543
This whole notion,

00:02:44.543 --> 00:02:46.549
the black family has
always been disintegrating,

00:02:46.549 --> 00:02:47.990
that is nonsense.

00:02:47.990 --> 00:02:49.629
His studies goes up to 1925,

00:02:49.629 --> 00:02:51.650
the great bulk of black families were

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:53.989
intact, two parent
families up through 1925

00:02:53.989 --> 00:02:56.900
and going all the way back
through the era of slavery.

00:02:56.900 --> 00:02:58.689
So it is now only within our own time

00:02:58.689 --> 00:03:02.200
that we suddenly see
this inevitable tragedy

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:04.630
which the welfare system says
is gonna rush in to solve.

00:03:04.630 --> 00:03:06.630
He was thinking like a Chicago economist.

00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:09.749
Essentially it's an
adherence to empiricism,

00:03:09.749 --> 00:03:13.369
to facts and logic and
putting that ahead of theory.

00:03:13.369 --> 00:03:14.809
Thomas was much more interested

00:03:14.809 --> 00:03:17.870
in how an idea has panned out.

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:20.029
What the evidence is
for success or failure

00:03:20.029 --> 00:03:22.750
rather than simply what the intent is.

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:24.699
And I think that is what distinguishes him

00:03:24.699 --> 00:03:26.789
and much of the Chicago school

00:03:26.789 --> 00:03:30.873
from their counterparts at MIT
or Harvard or other places,

00:03:30.873 --> 00:03:33.440
where there was much more of an emphasis,

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:34.529
at least in the field of economics,

00:03:34.529 --> 00:03:37.800
on eloquent theories and mathematics.

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:38.900
When Sowell showed up

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:41.250
at the University of
Chicago for doctoral work,

00:03:41.250 --> 00:03:43.090
he called himself a Marxist.

00:03:43.090 --> 00:03:45.190
A designation he ultimately rejected

00:03:45.190 --> 00:03:47.310
not because of Milton Friedman's lectures

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:49.774
but because of his
subsequent job experience

00:03:49.774 --> 00:03:51.224
in the Federal government.

00:03:51.224 --> 00:03:52.270
While I was still a Marxist,

00:03:52.270 --> 00:03:55.268
I was taking Milton
Friedman's course, but I...

00:03:55.268 --> 00:03:56.260
...

00:03:56.260 --> 00:03:59.407
But one summer in the government
was enough to let me say,

00:03:59.407 --> 00:04:02.070
"Now this government is
really not the answer."

00:04:02.070 --> 00:04:03.943
I mean that is.

00:04:03.943 --> 00:04:05.010
Milton Friedman didn't cure you

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:05.843
but the Federal government did.

00:04:05.843 --> 00:04:07.176
In fact the Federal government did.

00:04:07.176 --> 00:04:09.260
Never say the Federal
government doesn't do anything.

00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:10.840
One of Sowell's most brilliant works

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:13.870
is 'Knowledge and Decisions'
published in 1980.

00:04:13.870 --> 00:04:15.850
He elaborated on Austrian economist

00:04:15.850 --> 00:04:17.587
Friedrich Hayek's insight that

00:04:17.587 --> 00:04:20.280
information and knowledge
are disbursed among people

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:24.586
and markets in highly sophisticated
yet unarticulated ways.

00:04:24.586 --> 00:04:27.304
That's something most
intellectuals and planners ignore

00:04:27.304 --> 00:04:29.776
as they override the
expertise and experience

00:04:29.776 --> 00:04:32.700
of the common man through government fiat.

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:35.804
In a 1981 review of the
book published in 'Reason',

00:04:35.804 --> 00:04:37.237
Hayek wrote that Sowell had,

00:04:37.237 --> 00:04:40.060
"...not only broadened the
application of the ideas

00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:42.570
and effectively carried the
approach into new fields

00:04:42.570 --> 00:04:44.050
that I never considered,

00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:46.810
but he also succeeds
in translating abstract

00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:49.760
and theoretical argument
into a highly concrete

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:52.303
and realistic discussion
of the central problems

00:04:52.303 --> 00:04:54.870
of contemporary economic policy."

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:56.570
To the extent that decision making

00:04:56.570 --> 00:04:59.543
is taken out of the
hands of ordinary people

00:04:59.543 --> 00:05:01.874
and placed in the hands
of government bureaucrats

00:05:01.874 --> 00:05:02.970
and politicians.

00:05:02.970 --> 00:05:05.994
What you are doing is,
increasing the distance

00:05:05.994 --> 00:05:08.364
between the person who makes the decision

00:05:08.364 --> 00:05:10.844
and the person who has to
suffer the consequences

00:05:10.844 --> 00:05:12.140
of that decision.

00:05:12.140 --> 00:05:14.724
And one of the reasons Sowell
wrote that book is because

00:05:14.724 --> 00:05:17.374
he thought that too much decision making

00:05:17.374 --> 00:05:20.060
was being put in the hands of government

00:05:20.060 --> 00:05:23.100
and that we were headed down a bad path,

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:24.310
by going in this direction.

00:05:24.310 --> 00:05:27.010
And I think obviously he's
been borne out by that.

00:05:27.010 --> 00:05:28.390
In 'A Conflict of Visions'

00:05:28.390 --> 00:05:30.282
which is Sowell's
favorite of his own books,

00:05:30.282 --> 00:05:32.710
he identified two fundamental modes

00:05:32.710 --> 00:05:34.210
of understanding the world.

00:05:34.210 --> 00:05:36.938
Thinkers ranging from Rousseau
to Marx to John Rawls,

00:05:36.938 --> 00:05:40.298
exhibited what's Sowell
calls an unconstrained vision

00:05:40.298 --> 00:05:44.040
based on the mistaken idea
that human beings and society

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:45.200
are perfectible.

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:47.992
In later books Sowell
championed a constrained vision

00:05:47.992 --> 00:05:51.600
articulated by classical
liberals such as Adam Smith,

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:53.589
Edmund Burke and
America's founding fathers

00:05:53.589 --> 00:05:56.660
who constructed a system
of checks and balances

00:05:56.660 --> 00:05:59.500
to protect man from his worst tendencies.

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:01.947
The constrained vision rejects utopianism

00:06:01.947 --> 00:06:04.670
and instead holds that social progress

00:06:04.670 --> 00:06:05.910
comes from acknowledging that

00:06:05.910 --> 00:06:09.280
there are no perfect
solutions, only trade offs.

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:12.407
As Burke wrote, "We cannot
change the Nature of things

00:06:12.407 --> 00:06:16.610
and of men-but must act
upon them the best we can".

00:06:16.610 --> 00:06:18.954
Sowell's good friend and
fellow amateur photographer,

00:06:18.954 --> 00:06:21.091
Harvard linguist, Steven Pinker,

00:06:21.091 --> 00:06:24.084
draws a parallel between how cameras work

00:06:24.084 --> 00:06:26.047
and societies work.

00:06:29.130 --> 00:06:30.293
Thomas commentated to me that,

00:06:30.293 --> 00:06:33.483
to be a photographer, you
have to master trade offs.

00:06:35.030 --> 00:06:36.230
All of the little adjustments

00:06:36.230 --> 00:06:37.540
that you fiddle with in the camera,

00:06:37.540 --> 00:06:41.330
never involve making everything
better everything worse,

00:06:41.330 --> 00:06:43.300
it's a matter of trading
one thing off for another.

00:06:43.300 --> 00:06:46.200
If you close down the diaphragm,

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:49.143
then you get lots and lots of
stuff in focus near to far.

00:06:51.032 --> 00:06:53.950
On the other hand you're cutting
down the amount of light.

00:06:53.950 --> 00:06:56.133
And a theme in Tom's work on society

00:06:56.133 --> 00:06:59.010
is that all policies involve trade offs.

00:06:59.010 --> 00:07:01.320
And Tom often loses patience at people

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:04.110
with sweeping visions, kere's
how we can improve society,

00:07:04.110 --> 00:07:06.250
here's a solution to a problem.

00:07:06.250 --> 00:07:09.033
And Tom points outs in
his political writings,

00:07:09.033 --> 00:07:12.020
there are no solutions,
there are only trade offs.

00:07:12.020 --> 00:07:15.020
Sowell's life experience
and ideological commitments

00:07:15.020 --> 00:07:17.628
are perhaps most clear in
his long held positions

00:07:17.628 --> 00:07:20.090
on the centrality of school choice

00:07:20.090 --> 00:07:22.830
to improving the life
prospects for low income blacks

00:07:22.830 --> 00:07:24.230
and other minorities.

00:07:24.230 --> 00:07:27.347
One of his first major works was 1972's,

00:07:27.347 --> 00:07:30.170
'Black Education Myths and Tragedies'

00:07:30.170 --> 00:07:32.507
and his latest book, was 2020's,

00:07:32.507 --> 00:07:34.390
'Charter Schools and Their Enemies'

00:07:34.390 --> 00:07:37.600
I would allow their
parents to have a choice

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:38.660
of where to send them to school,

00:07:38.660 --> 00:07:40.580
whether that choice is
called a voucher scheme,

00:07:40.580 --> 00:07:42.690
open enrollment, tuition tax credit,

00:07:42.690 --> 00:07:44.450
any kind of scheme of that sort,

00:07:44.450 --> 00:07:46.983
that would put that power in
the hands of their parents.

00:07:46.983 --> 00:07:49.390
Mainly because that would
mean that the schools

00:07:49.390 --> 00:07:50.883
would have to be responsive to them.

00:07:50.883 --> 00:07:52.942
As it is now the school is a monopoly.

00:07:52.942 --> 00:07:54.523
They need not be responsive.

00:07:54.523 --> 00:07:58.920
It is hard for me to understand
what harm is gonna be done

00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:00.728
by allowing parents to have a choice

00:08:00.728 --> 00:08:03.725
as compared to having self
interested bureaucrats

00:08:03.725 --> 00:08:05.020
have a monopoly.

00:08:05.020 --> 00:08:06.820
Tom brings this economic empiricism

00:08:06.820 --> 00:08:08.746
to anything he's writing about.

00:08:08.746 --> 00:08:13.160
That's something that
I've greatly appreciated

00:08:13.160 --> 00:08:15.943
and then the need for,
to be a critical thinker,

00:08:17.150 --> 00:08:18.390
in the way that he has.

00:08:18.390 --> 00:08:20.760
He wrote about these racial topics

00:08:20.760 --> 00:08:23.780
almost out of a sense of duty
because there were things

00:08:23.780 --> 00:08:26.470
that needed to be said that too few others

00:08:26.470 --> 00:08:27.383
were willing to say.

00:08:27.383 --> 00:08:31.262
And I'm so glad he did his duty.

00:08:31.262 --> 00:08:33.963
I think we are so much better off,

00:08:33.963 --> 00:08:37.522
for having this body of
work out there to draw on,

00:08:37.522 --> 00:08:39.741
that is still so relevant today.

00:08:39.741 --> 00:08:42.670
If he had spent his entire
career writing about

00:08:42.670 --> 00:08:45.600
intellectual history, Jean-Baptiste Say

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:47.670
and the classical liberals,

00:08:47.670 --> 00:08:50.640
I'm sure he'd still be
remembered as a great scholar

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:54.560
but I am so glad he decided
to take the punches he's taken

00:08:54.560 --> 00:08:58.420
over this other stuff on race
and ethnicity and culture.

00:08:58.420 --> 00:09:01.222
Because I think for some of us,

00:09:02.990 --> 00:09:04.790
that's equally important.

00:09:04.790 --> 00:09:08.320
Despite disavowing utopianism
and human perfectibility,

00:09:08.320 --> 00:09:10.290
Sowell has never wavered in a belief

00:09:10.290 --> 00:09:12.055
that things can and should get better

00:09:12.055 --> 00:09:14.566
if we put aside ideological priors,

00:09:14.566 --> 00:09:16.735
assess the facts on the ground

00:09:16.735 --> 00:09:20.550
and work to give individuals
more and better choices.

00:09:20.550 --> 00:09:23.190
I grew up in an era when
people and particularly blacks,

00:09:23.190 --> 00:09:25.060
were a lot poorer than today,

00:09:25.060 --> 00:09:27.231
faced a lot more
discrimination than today.

00:09:27.231 --> 00:09:29.390
And in which the teenage pregnancy rate

00:09:29.390 --> 00:09:31.690
was a lot lower than today.

00:09:31.690 --> 00:09:33.930
I don't believe there
is a predestined amount

00:09:33.930 --> 00:09:34.785
of teenage pregnancy,

00:09:34.785 --> 00:09:37.340
a predestined amount of husband desertion.

00:09:37.340 --> 00:09:39.080
Today Sowell is in his 90s,

00:09:39.080 --> 00:09:40.825
and still writing and publishing.

00:09:40.825 --> 00:09:43.590
Though his greatest scholarship is almost

00:09:43.590 --> 00:09:44.625
certainly behind him,

00:09:44.625 --> 00:09:47.825
his body of work will continue
to have a profound impact

00:09:47.825 --> 00:09:51.483
on our understanding of the
world, long after he's gone.

